# Q: can a mission declare a side the victory rule does not judge — and is the same mission refused
#    when the declaration is missing?
#
#   $exe = "src/MiVic.Game/bin/Debug/net9.0/MiVic.Game.exe"
#   $p = Start-Process -FilePath $exe -Wait -PassThru -NoNewWindow -WorkingDirectory . -ArgumentList `
#       '--paperclip-demo', '--probe', 'tools/probe/paperclip.probe', `
#       '--probe-out', 'artifacts/probe/paperclip.txt'
#
# Three layers read a world and were never asked about the world a mission *opens* in. The trigger
# layer was the first (`tools/probe/triggers.probe`), the objectives the second
# (`tools/probe/objectives.probe`), and the victory rule is the third. It has been safe by
# construction rather than by being asked — the scenario lays a base down for every team a match
# declares — and the mission this script runs is the one that construction cannot express: ROADMAP §8's
# Operation Paperclip, where Δυτικοί agents come for the scientists of a remote outpost. The scientists
# are an objective and not an army: no base, no structures, no ground of their own. A declared side
# standing in nothing is exactly what the rule cannot tell from a side that has been destroyed, so
# without a word from the match it is read as already beaten — and where such a side is the only one
# off the player's, the match is won on the check that first asks it.
#
# The word is `Judged = false` on the team's declaration, and the pair of missions this script reads is
# the whole of the demonstration:
#
#   paperclip         the mission as it should be. The outpost's team is declared a side the victory
#                     rule does not judge, so it is a design rather than a mistake: it validates clean
#                     and plays. Its objectives are sound — a denial with a deadline inside the time
#                     limit, and a command-centre constraint with no deadline, which is the shape that
#                     must stay exempt.
#   paperclip_draft   the same mission as its author first wrote it, with three mistakes in it: the
#                     declaration missing, the constraint without the word that makes it a constraint,
#                     and a denial deadline of 9 000 ticks against a mission that has 7 200.
#
# Nothing the campaign ships trips any of the three checks, which is the point of them and also the
# reason their refusals could never be read anywhere.
#
# **This run is supposed to end with one check failed.** That failing check is the validator refusing
# the draft, with its reasons printed above it; a run that passed here would mean the third layer had
# stopped looking.
#
# What to read:
#
#   - `validate` with no id asks about the mission the match is playing, which is the sound one: three
#     teams in the match, no problem at all. `teams` beside it is the same fact from the side of the
#     world — the `match` line names the outpost's team as *a side the victory rule does not judge*,
#     which is the one part of a declaration that is not a faction, and the outpost has nothing else
#     to be recognised by.
#   - `validate paperclip_draft` is the refusal, in three sentences, and each names what it found:
#     side 2 stands in no structures in the world the mission opens in, so the rule reads it as already
#     beaten — and its verdict on that world is `victory` already; the command centre objective is not
#     a constraint and has no deadline, so nothing could ever complete it; and the denial's deadline of
#     9 000 arrives after the match is over, so it can never be reached. The three are reported
#     together because they are three separate authoring mistakes and the author has to fix all three.
#   - `tick 1` is the mission *playing*: the outpost's people are on the map — six unarmed Συλλέκτες of
#     a side that owns no ground — and the match is `ongoing`, which is what it would not be if the
#     declaration were missing. They stand where the mission's script put them, because a definition
#     has no column for a fourth team and a side with no base is not a side the AI plays. The `units`
#     line asks for them by *faction* and not by team, and that is the trap rather than a slip: a bare
#     numeral in that argument is read as a faction first (see the command table), so `units 3` would
#     list the Δυτικοί and not team 3. The team is named in the `match` line and nowhere else.
#   - `tick 200` and `tick 1000` are the same reading later: the match is still being played, the
#     outpost's team still holds nothing, and the objective that decides the mission is still open.
#     That is the difference between a fix that enables a mission and one that only stops it being
#     refused.
#   - the shot is the player's view of it: three sides on the panel — Σοβιετικοί, Δυτικοί and the
#     outpost's Κινέζοι, the last of them with nothing built — over ground the agents have to cross.

# --- the mission as it should be: a side that holds objectives rather than ground -------------------------------
validate
teams

# --- and the same mission as its author first wrote it: the same cast, without the word -------------------------
validate paperclip_draft

# --- the first tick: the outpost's people are on the map, and nothing has been decided --------------------------
tick 1
teams
units chinese
objectives

# --- ten seconds in: the column is walking, and the match is still being played ---------------------------------
tick 200
teams
objectives

# --- a minute in: still nothing decided for the player -----------------------------------------------------------
tick 1000
teams

# --- what the player sees: three sides, one of them holding nothing ----------------------------------------------
hud on
focus 250 0
zoom 300
pitch -0.85
settle 20
shot artifacts/probe/paperclip-sides.png

# --- and the six themselves, close enough to count ---------------------------------------------------------------
hud off
zoom 110
pitch -0.55
settle 20
shot artifacts/probe/paperclip-outpost.png
